.TH SET_INSERTSA 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
set_insertsa \- Insert a string allocation into a set

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/set.h>
.IB #include <lib/stralloc.h>

.IB int
.IB set_insertsa(set_t* set, const stralloc* sa);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBset_insertsa\fR function inserts a string allocation into a set. It takes two arguments: the set and the string allocation. The function returns 1 if the string allocation is successfully inserted, 2 if the string allocation already exists in the set, and -1 if an error occurs.

.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.IB set
The set into which to insert the string allocation.
.TP
.IB sa
The string allocation to insert.

.SH RETURN VALUE
The function returns 1 if the string allocation is successfully inserted, 2 if the string allocation already exists in the set, and -1 if an error occurs.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBset_insertsa\fR function:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/set.h>
#include <lib/stralloc.h>

int main() {
    set_t set;
    stralloc sa;
    stralloc_init(&sa);
    stralloc_append(&sa, "apple");

    set_init(&set);
    if (set_insertsa(&set, &sa) == 1) {
        printf("String allocation inserted successfully\n");
    } else if (set_insertsa(&set, &sa) == 2) {
        printf("String allocation already exists in set\n");
    } else {
        printf("Error inserting string allocation\n");
    }

    set_free(&set);
    stralloc_free(&sa);
    return 0;
}
.nef